WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.735
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:05.869
we help some DNA technicians
improve their upload speeds.

00:00:05.869 --> 00:00:07.660
Will they get the folds
out of their genome

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:08.830
before their big meeting?

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:12.130
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.040
DNA SOAP is a biotech
company whose main product

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.590
is a software that does protein
folding across distributed

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:18.370
machines.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:21.280
Sadly, though, any time they
would provide a new data set

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.260
to be worked on, there
was a significant delay

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.110
before any clients would
start doing computation.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.270
Their architecture was
pretty straightforward.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.700
Once a new genome
data set came in,

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.950
it would get chopped up and
uploaded to Google Cloud

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.040
Storage, where clients, who were
listening for pub/sub updates,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.800
would grab the work blocks
and start computing.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.360
Now, here's the
interesting part.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.340
The size of the
work blocks changed,

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.470
depending on how many
connected clients there

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:45.940
were to do the work.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.790
So more workers resulted
in smaller work blocks

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.600
to balance out properly.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.020
The problem at hand was
that the DNA SOAP software

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.650
was taking a really long time
to upload all the files to GCS

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.550
in regions with a really high
number of available workers.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.640
To try and replicate this, I
created a small Python script,

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.830
which generated files
of various sizes,

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:08.830
and then uploaded each file
to a GCS regional bucket

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.360
about 100 times or so.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.850
Turns out, the smaller
the files in your set,

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.212
the slower your upload speed.

00:01:14.212 --> 00:01:15.670
The reason for this
is that there's

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.800
a certain amount of
transactional overhead

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.160
for uploading files.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.630
So the smaller they are, the
more this overhead adds up.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:23.620
The solution?

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.320
Parallel uploads.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.170
gsutil provides the -m option,
which performs parallel

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.180
copy which can
significantly increase

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.440
the performance of an upload.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.565
Now, when we chart the
performance against our base

00:01:34.565 --> 00:01:36.880
state, we can see that the
parallel version provides

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.680
significantly higher
throughput since

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.700
the transactional overhead is
mitigated across many threats.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.040
For DNA SOAP, this
small command line flag

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.430
resulted in a 2x improvement in
upload speed for their files,

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.940
not bad for a bunch
of scientists.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.940
If you want to know more
about parallel uploads,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.730
check out the associated article
listed in the description

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:55.340
below.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.040
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.360
Performance Atlas content.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.397
When it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

00:02:01.397 --> 00:02:05.569
